 \ReviewerComment{3. For some reason you picked lsp instead of convective precipitation. I realize that picking both is meaningless because tp is a sum of the two, but, from the practical point of view, summer convective cases are typically harder to predict. Did you deliberately focus on large-scale precipitation?}
 
-\PendingResponse
+\AuthorResponse{We thank the reviewer for this question. The original manuscript did not explain why lsp, rather than convective precipitation (cp), appears among the predictors. We did not deliberately focus on large-scale precipitation, and the revised manuscript now states this explicitly.
 
-\ChangeLocation
+\textit{Why lsp and not cp.} The predictors were selected from an 11-variable candidate pool by the absolute contemporaneous Spearman correlation with total precipitation over the training period; this was a same-time screening, not a lead-time one. Apart from the tp target, the pool consisted of the ERA5 single-level fields retrieved for the original study. Convective precipitation was not retrieved as a separate field and was therefore never screened. lsp entered the final set because it ranked third in that screening ($\lvert\rho\rvert=0.899$), not because the study was designed around large-scale precipitation. In the later training-only diagnostic with an expanded pool, cp had a contemporaneous correlation of 0.843 and would have ranked fourth (Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r2m3_associations}). Its omission from the original pool was therefore not a judgement about its relevance.
+
+\textit{Target.} The target has always been total precipitation, including its convective part, and the convective cases are part of every evaluation. mxtpr and mntpr are rates of total precipitation, so recent convective precipitation is also partly represented in the inputs. We agree that same-hour lsp and cp are redundant given tp, since $\mathrm{tp}=\mathrm{lsp}+\mathrm{cp}$. For lagged inputs, however, whether cp adds predictive information is an empirical question, and we tested it in two ways.
+
+\textit{Does cp improve the forecasts?} First, adding regional-mean $\max(\mathrm{tp}-\mathrm{lsp},0)$ to the seven predictors as an eighth XGBoost input changed mean overall RMSE from 0.243920 to 0.243693~mm\,h$^{-1}$: a paired difference of $-0.000227$~mm\,h$^{-1}$ ($-0.093\%$; pointwise 95\% seed-t interval $[-0.001643,+0.001189]$), with lower RMSE in three of five seeds. Second, in the matched C6 test, replacing d2m with cp within the seven-predictor budget changed overall RMSE by $+0.000132$~mm\,h$^{-1}$ ($+0.054\%$) for XGBoost, with both intervals including zero, and by $+0.001113$~mm\,h$^{-1}$ ($+0.458\%$) for BiLSTM-Attention, with higher RMSE in all five seeds and both intervals excluding zero. The two tests answer different questions (adding cp, and replacing d2m by cp); we did not run a test that replaces lsp with cp. Neither showed a consistent gain from cp under these configurations. This does not show that cp has no predictive value, nor that lsp is the best precipitation predictor.
+
+\textit{Convective cases.} We agree that convective precipitation is typically harder to predict, and we have added a stratified evaluation to Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:seasonal_baseline_check} and Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r4conv_strata}. Of the 854 wet test targets ($\geq0.1$~mm\,h$^{-1}$), 383 were convective-dominated ($\mathrm{cp}/\mathrm{tp}\geq0.5$ in the target hour) and 471 large-scale-dominated; 146 and 114 of them fell in JJA. This classification uses the target hour after the fact and is not available to the models when a forecast is issued. RMSE skill relative to the training month-by-hour climatology was lower for convective-dominated targets in all six models; for XGBoost it was 0.091 versus 0.166, and 0.103 versus 0.167 within JJA. This supports the reviewer's expectation for convective-dominated hours. It does not extend to summer as a whole: XGBoost skill was 0.127 in JJA and 0.099 outside it, and JJA skill was higher in five of six models. Other metrics also ordered the strata differently; for example, RMSE relative to the subset mean was slightly lower for convective-dominated targets. As reported in our response to Major Comment~2, lsp accounts for 50.92\% of JJA precipitation, so about half of summer precipitation is convective in ERA5; it remains part of the target and of the evaluation. The stratum differences were not tested statistically and are descriptive.
+
+These results remain conditional on ERA5 reanalysis, whose large-scale/convective split reflects its parameterization; on the 25-cell regional mean, which smooths small convective cells; on six-hourly sampled inputs (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:limitations}); and on one test period, with single saved runs for the stratified evaluation and one or two architectures for the cp tests.}
+
+\ManuscriptChange{Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:predictor_selection_results} (Predictor Selection Results): one paragraph added after the C6 results, stating that cp was not in the original candidate pool, that lsp was retained by screening rather than to target large-scale precipitation, that the target is total precipitation, and reporting the cp-addition XGBoost check. Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:seasonal_baseline_check} (Descriptive Seasonal Check): one paragraph added at the end on convective- and large-scale-dominated target strata and on JJA versus non-JJA skill. Supplementary: new final section with Table~\ref{supp-stab:r4conv_strata}. The contemporaneous screening description in Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:dataset} and the C6 results (Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r2m3_c6}) are unchanged. The time semantics and seasonal lsp shares added for Major Comment~2 are not repeated.}
+
